    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n\n\nWork on $x>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/3}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/3+1}}{(\\frac{7}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{10/3}}{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{10/3}}{10}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{10/3}}{10}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{10/3}}{10}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{10/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{10}{3}{x}^{10/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
